WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:00.667
The attorney

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:01.601
representing Trump

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.569
in the Justice Department

00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:03.970
probe into the classified documents

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:04.337
found at Mar

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:04.971
a Lago

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:06.239
took highly detailed notes

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:07.841
about their conversations.

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:09.142
And sources tell CNN

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:10.410
the notes included details

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.978
about Trump's desire

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:13.646
to push back to fight the Justice

00:00:13.646 --> 00:00:15.482
Department effort through subpoena.

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:17.550
To recover those documents, those notes.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.852
This is the important part

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:20.854
now in the hands of the special counsel.

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:22.589
Katelyn Polantz is here to break that down

00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:23.289
for us.

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:24.724
Their evidentiary value,

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:26.292
I assume, is rather large.

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:27.060
Yeah.
So, John,

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.862
you have a criminal investigation

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:29.796
and what is crucial

00:00:29.796 --> 00:00:31.131
in any criminal investigation

00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:32.766
is how good is your evidence?

00:00:32.766 --> 00:00:33.700
The evidence here

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.702
appears to be very good.

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:37.237
It is very detailed

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:38.905
to the point where members of the Trump

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:40.306
team were surprised

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:42.008
at how detailed these notes are.

00:00:42.008 --> 00:00:43.309
And these are notes

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:44.511
that speak to exactly

00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:45.512
what the Justice Department

00:00:45.512 --> 00:00:47.414
wants to do as they investigate

00:00:47.414 --> 00:00:48.648
whether or not

00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:50.283
Donald Trump himself

00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:50.850
was trying

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.652
to obstruct their investigation.

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:54.788
So in May of 20, 22,

00:00:55.288 --> 00:00:56.823
he and his attorney will

00:00:56.823 --> 00:00:58.091
receive the subpoena

00:00:58.091 --> 00:00:59.025
saying, you got to give back

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:00.160
all the classified records

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.261
that you have in your possession.

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:01.995
Anything marked

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:03.797
with a classification on it,

00:01:03.797 --> 00:01:05.732
give it back to the grand jury.

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:08.034
And then these notes are Evan Corcoran's

00:01:08.034 --> 00:01:09.235
as a defense attorney,

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:10.203
and they cover

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:11.638
detailed conversations

00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:13.740
directly between Evan Corcoran.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:14.707
Donald Trump,

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:16.242
the types of conversations

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:17.610
that when the Justice Department

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:18.545
sought these notes,

00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:19.779
when they went to court to fight

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:20.947
to get access to them,

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:21.848
they believed

00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:23.583
would show Donald Trump

00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:25.718
trying to commit a crime.

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:26.853
And they have these notes

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:27.454
now, and they are

00:01:27.454 --> 00:01:28.621
quite, quite

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:30.123
significant in the fact

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:31.424
that they would be so detailed.

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.193
And we also are getting a little snippet

00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:35.295
of what the substance of these notes are.

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:37.697
And we can see that Donald Trump,

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:38.331
or at least we're hearing

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:39.065
from our sources

00:01:39.065 --> 00:01:39.933
that Donald Trump

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:41.534
was raising the possibility,

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:43.169
do we have to comply with this subpoena?

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:44.471
We don't know how that fits.

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:46.439
But it certainly does factor

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:48.041
into this obstruction investigation.

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:49.309
Shan Wu is still with us.

00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:50.977
Help us understand where it might fit.

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:52.312
And again, we have to be careful.

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:53.346
But you have detailed

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:55.648
notes from an attorney, a good attorney.

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:56.749
And so if you're asking

00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:58.051
if you're telling your client,

00:01:58.051 --> 00:01:58.685
the former president,

00:01:58.685 --> 00:01:59.919
we have this subpoena,

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:01.521
the subpoena is valid.

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:03.389
We have to comply with the subpoena.

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:04.457
I assume that the attorney

00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:05.091
is walking through this.

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:07.026
If you get a response from the client

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:08.628
saying no, don't do it.

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:10.063
What does that tell you?

00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:10.430
Well,

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:12.632
it tells you that the client doesn't

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:14.234
want to comply with that subpoena

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:16.302
and maybe try to hide something.

00:02:16.436 --> 00:02:18.371
I think the critical aspect to me here,

00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:19.205
and we don't know this

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:20.173
yet from the details,

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:21.141
is whether or not

00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:22.008
this is evidence

00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:23.042
that Trump

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:25.111
was basically admitting to his attorney.

00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:27.080
I know I have classified documents

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.715
and I don't want to give them back.

00:02:28.715 --> 00:02:31.017
That would be a very helpful thing

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:32.152
to the Justice Department now.

00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:32.986
Trump's defense to

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:33.686
this is going to be I'm

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:34.988
just asking for legal advice

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:35.922
and I want to know,

00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:38.224
can I push back on the subpoena or not?

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:39.592
I will say one thing.

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:41.661
It strikes me as a little bit unusual

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:43.796
that Corcoran's notes are this detailed.

00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:46.232
When I was a prosecutor,

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:47.534
prosecutors different their styles.

00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:48.568
But sometimes you don't

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:50.069
want to take a whole lot of notes

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:51.237
because you're always worried

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:53.506
that somebody may try to get them

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:54.541
to question, you know,

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:55.875
what kind of contemporaneous thoughts

00:02:55.875 --> 00:02:57.277
were going on there.

00:02:57.277 --> 00:02:58.244
So it's possible

00:02:58.244 --> 00:02:59.312
Corcoran was doing this

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:00.513
as a little bit of a safety

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:02.048
net for himself,

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:04.050
knowing that this is a difficult client

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:04.951
who is kind of volatile.

00:03:04.951 --> 00:03:06.352
He may have been really documenting

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.121
his contemporaneous notes at the time.

00:03:08.121 --> 00:03:09.088
That level of detail

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:11.524
reminds me more of an FBI

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:13.293
agents notes than it does.

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:15.328
And also notes at this moment,

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:16.829
Corcoran is talking to

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:17.263
his client,

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:17.964
Donald Trump,

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:20.500
about collecting documents at Mar a Lago.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:21.501
And when he

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:23.369
hands over an envelope in June,

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:24.737
which is the end of these notes,

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:26.172
the Justice Department got

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:27.340
he has an envelope.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.142
It has 38 documents

00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:30.577
with classified markings on it.

00:03:30.577 --> 00:03:32.212
And he tells the Justice Department

00:03:32.212 --> 00:03:33.213
at this point,

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:34.881
we know this from court filings

00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:36.149
that he believes

00:03:36.149 --> 00:03:38.218
there was no other classified documents

00:03:38.218 --> 00:03:39.152
at Mar a Lago.

00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:41.254
Obviously, there were hundreds of more.

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:42.121
And so the justice system

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:43.156
is trying to figure out,

00:03:43.156 --> 00:03:44.290
well, who knew about that

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:45.592
and how did this happen,

00:03:45.592 --> 00:03:47.760
if this attorney performed this search,

00:03:48.328 --> 00:03:49.629
having detailed notes

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:49.963
about what

00:03:49.963 --> 00:03:51.998
those conversations were between

00:03:51.998 --> 00:03:52.699
Evan Corcoran

00:03:52.699 --> 00:03:53.333
and Donald Trump,

00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:55.268
perhaps could answer that question

00:03:55.268 --> 00:03:56.869
or bring them closer to an answer.

00:03:56.869 --> 00:03:58.638
And Evan Corcoran,

00:03:58.638 --> 00:03:59.906
to wrap
this up, it's also been before

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:01.140
the grand jury, correct?

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:01.708
Yes. Right.

00:04:01.708 --> 00:04:03.376
So this gets interesting.

00:04:03.376 --> 00:04:04.510
Fascinating, complicated.

